abstract:
Twenty-five percent of the Top500 (the 500 largest High-Performance Computers (HPCs) in the world) are used at academic institutions to drive a range of computational research from AI to fusion to radiotherapy. 90% of these systems, along with many UK HPCs, use the Slurm Workload Manager to schedule jobs run on the system through a queueing system. Slurm is 500,000 lines of code with a dozen different ways to affect job priority and queue position, where shaping your job the wrong way can leave it stranded in the queue delaying your research. I have had the opportunity to study the way Slurm jobs queue and I can now share what and how I've learned about how Slurm prioritises jobs while designing the University of Sheffield's HPC High-Priority Service. Together we'll:
explore how Slurm decides what to run next using multidimensional Tetris;
discover backfilling and its limits;
learn of the 7 types of priority;
investigate how "fair" FairShare really is;
see when to let it cook; and
learn how this can apply to the HPC you use.
Come away from this presentation with the tools to understand Slurm job queueing and techniques to help craft your job to queue quickest.
